# 3 readings by claude-opus-5-5, compared
hume-induction.md:3: argue/readings: S6 is read 2 ways: hume-induction.logic.r3 has nothing from it; hume-induction.logic.r3.2 and hume-induction.logic.r3.3 have "arg_exists → moral_arg"; they differ when only "there is an argument engaging us to trust past experience and make it the standard of our future judgement" is true, which the first allows
hume-induction.md:3: argue/answered: S6: answered no, which keeps hume-induction.logic.r3.2 and hume-induction.logic.r3.3, to: Suppose "there is an argument engaging us to trust past experience and make it the standard of our future judgement" is true and "there is a probable or moral argument (concerning matter of fact and existence) that the future will be conformable to the past" is false. Is S6, "If we be, therefore, engaged by arguments to put trust in past experience, and make it the standard of our future judgement, these arguments must be probable only, or such as regard matter of fact and real existence, according to the division above mentioned.", true then?
hume-induction.md:3: argue/readings: S8 is read 2 ways: hume-induction.logic.r3 has "moral_arg → presupposes_uniformity"; hume-induction.logic.r3.2 and hume-induction.logic.r3.3 have "presupposes_uniformity"; they differ when nothing is true, which the first allows
hume-induction.md:3: argue/answered: S8: answered no, which keeps hume-induction.logic.r3.2 and hume-induction.logic.r3.3, to: Suppose "there is a probable or moral argument (concerning matter of fact and existence) that the future will be conformable to the past" and "arguments concerning existence proceed upon the supposition that the future will be conformable to the past" are false. Is S8, "We have said that all arguments concerning existence are founded on the relation of cause and effect; that our knowledge of that relation is derived entirely from experience; and that all our experimental conclusions proceed upon the supposition that the future will be conformable to the past.", true then?
